The widely used melt-blown polypropylene (PP) non-woven fabrics had no antibacterial functions and its largescale use also increased the burden on the environment owing to its non-degradable property. Herein, silver (I) metal organic frameworks $(\mathsf{A g-2M I})$ were prepared with ${\tt A g N O}_{3}$ and 2-methylimidazole and embedded into degradable poly(L-lactide) (PLLA) to make an ultrathin filtration and antibacterial membrane by electrospinning technology with low loading of Ag-2MI. The morphology, mechanical properties, adsorption performance and antibacterial activities of the prepared films were tested and the results indicated that the addition of Ag-2MI could reduce the diameter of PLLA fibers from $910\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $520~\mathrm{nm}$ 1 ${\cdot}1.8\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ of ${\tt A g-}2{\tt M I})$ , while the tensile strength, elongation at break of the membrane and the contact angle of the films were enhanced. Although the thickness of the prepared membranes was only about one-third of that of commercially available melt-blown cloth, they exhibited better filtering performances than the melt-blown cloth. The fiber membrane with low loading of $1.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Ag-2MI showed $99.99\%$ inhibition rate against Escherichia coli and Staphylococcus aureus.
## 1. Introduction
Air pollution from various suspended particulate matter (PM) can easily induce asthma, lung cancer and various cardiovascular diseases such as hypertension, heart failure and myocardial infarction [1]. In recent years, highly infectious diseases such as SARS and the coronavi­ rus disease 2019 (COVID-19) have become major issues threatening human health. Melt-blown polypropylene (PP) non-woven fabrics used as the filtration material play a crucial role in preventing air pollution and severe infectious diseases such as SARS and COVID-19 [2–4]. However, the melt-blown PP non-woven fabrics do not have functions such as antibacterial properties and are difficult to degrade in the environment [5]. The disposal of them after use will cause environ­ mental pollution. With the improvement of people’s health awareness and the strengthening of environmental protection, functional and degradable filter materials have indeed attracted people’s attention [6].
Electrospinning is the effective fabrication technique to obtain filter materials with functionalities and high performances at an industrialscale through polymer blending, adding functional fillers in matrix or controlling fiber morphology [7–12]. Jing [13] prepared poly­ acrylonitrile (PAN) fibers modified with ionic liquid DEAP by electrospinning and the PM2.5 adsorption capacity of the PAN/DEAP sample was significantly higher than that of the pure PAN film. Selvam [14] et al. prepared antibacterial filters by incorporating diverse weight percentages of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ nanoparticles in electrospinning PAN fibers. The re­ sults showed that the electrospun PAN fibers with Ag nanoparticles were successfully against S. aureus and E. coli with increased $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ content. The $10{-}12.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ content of Ag was sufficient to reach $99\%$ bacterial filtration efficiency.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are porous crystalline materials constructed from organic linkers and metal ions/clusters. MOFs have emerged as promising materials for various antimicrobial applications owing to their different functions, including the controlled/stimulated decomposition of components with bactericidal activity, strong in­ teractions with bacterial membranes, and formation of photogenerated reactive oxygen species (ROS) as well as high loading and sustained releasing capacities for other antimicrobial materials [15–17]. Ag-based MOFs has received more and more attention due to the high antimi­ crobial efficiency of ${\mathbf{A}}{\mathbf{g}}^{+}$ [17–20]. Owing to high specific surface area, pore volume, porosity, easily tunable framework structure and ratio­ nally immobilized functional sites, MOFs also occupied an important position in the field of adsorption and filtration materials [21,22]. Zhang [23] prepared a fibrous membrane that could effectively kill bacteria by embedding silver metal organic frameworks $\mathbf{Ag_{2}(H B T C)}$ into degradable polylactic acid through electrospinning. The composite filters of silver metal organic frameworks/cellulose fibers (Ag-MOFs@CF) were pre­ pared through an in situ deposition method by Ma [24] et al. Excellent antimicrobial activity against Escherichia coli (E. coli) with an inhibition zone diameter of $20.8\mathrm{mm}$ was obtained with the loading of Ag-MOFs up to $37.04~\mathrm{wt\%}$ .
In this study, we prepared a kind of Ag-MOFs $(\mathsf{A g-2M I})$ with ${\tt A g N O}_{3}$ and 2-methylimidazole, and combined it with poly(L-lactide) (PLLA) to prepare PLLA/Ag-2MI composite fiber membranes for air pollution control and medical protection through electrospinning method. The electrospinning fiber membranes had been considered as the next gen­ eration material for air pollution control and medical protection, which could be thinner than that of melt-blown non-woven fabrics of PP in the market because of their tunable orifice size and high surface area to mass. A small amount of Ag-2MI gave the prepared membrane good antibacterial properties. The lower added amount of $\mathsf{A g-}2\mathrm{MI}$ and the thinner filter membranes could reduce the cost of film and the usage of polymer. Since PLLA was biodegradable and its raw materials were renewable, the prepared membranes were environmentally friendly and degradable, which could reduce the environment burden. The properties of the prepared membranes had been systematically investigated and the related mechanisms had been discussed.
## 2. Materials and method
## 2.1. Materials
Poly(L-lactide) (REVODE290, PLLA) with a melt flow rate of 10–30 $g/\mathrm{min}$ was purchased from Zhejiang Hai Zheng Biomaterials Co., Ltd. ${\tt A g N O}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{5}O H}$ (AR), $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ (AR, DCM), $^{N,N}$ -dimethylformamide (AR, DMF) and nutrient agar (BR) were all supplied by Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. 2-Methylimidazole $98\%$ , 2MI) was pur­ chased from Nanjing Jiaoziteng Scientific Equipment Co., Ltd. Escher­ ichia coli (E. coli) and Staphylococcus aureus (S. aureus) were from the School of Environment and Biology, Nanjing University of Science and Technology.
## 2.2. Synthesis of the silver metal organic framework (Ag-2MI)
Ag-2MI was synthesized according to the method by Pejman [25] et al. $0.6\ g\mathrm{AgNO_{3}}$ was added into $90\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water to prepare a salt solution. At the same time, $1.05~\mathrm{g}$ of 2MI was added to $90~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol to prepare a ligand solution. Then, the ligand solution was slowly poured into the salt solution and stirred for $3.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . The $\mathsf{A g-}2\mathrm{MI}$ powders were centrifuged and washed with ethanol for three times (3 $\times10~\mathrm{mL}$ ). Subsequently, the product was washed twice with DMF $(2\times$ $10~\mathrm{mL}$ ). Finally, Ag-2MI powders were dispersed in DMF to prepare Ag2MI/DMF suspension.
## 2.3. Preparation of PLLA/Ag-2MI electrospinning composite membrane
PLLA and DCM were added to a round-bottomed flask, and the mixture was sealed and stirred for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to dissolve PLLA in DCM completely. Then, the suspension of $\mathtt{A g-2M I}$ in DMF (The Ag-2MI par­ ticles were dispersed in DMF by an ultrasonic mixing for $30\mathrm{minj}$ ) was put into the above round-bottomed flask and stirred for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ to obtain the electrospinning solution. Table 1 listed the solution compositions under investigation.
The above-mentioned spinning solution $\mathrm{(4~mL)}$ was put into a $\mathrm{5~mL}$ plastics syringe. The syringe was installed on the electrostatic spinning machine. The electrospinning conditions were set as follows: the pump pushing speed was $0.0040\mathrm{mm}/s$ , the receiving distance was $17\mathrm{cm}$ , the drum speed was $70~\mathrm{rpm}$ , the high-voltage was $20\mathrm{~kV}$ , the temperature was $30{-}35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and the humidity was $60{-}80\%\mathrm{RH}$ . Finally, the PLLA/Ag
2MI membranes with a thickness of about $0.0036~\mathrm{mm}$ were prepared. We named the membranes containing 0, 1, 1.4, and $1.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathtt{A g-2M I}$ as PLLA, PAM1.0, PAM1.4 and PAM1.8 in sequence.
## 2.4. General characterizations
The field emission scanning electron microscope of FEI company (Quanta 250FEG) was used to observe the surface morphology of Ag2MI and membranes. X-ray diffraction (XRD) was performed on D8- ADVANCE X-ray diffractometer from Bruker (Germany). The Kα $\left(\lambda=\right.$ $1.5418\mathrm{nm}\rangle$ was used as the radiation source with the scanning range of $5\mathrm{-}70^{\circ}$ . The tube current was $40\mathrm{mA}$ and the tube voltage was $40\mathrm{kV}$ . The Fourier infrared (FTIR) test was carried with FTIR-8400S infrared spectrum analyzer of Shimadzu (Japan). The KBr tablet method was used for the Ag-2MI test and the scanning range was $400{\cdot}4000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was conducted by the GA/ SDTA85 thermogravimetric analyzer at $40\ \mathrm{mL/min}$ of nitrogen flow from room temperature to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a heating rate of $10~\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ . The amount of sample was $2{-}3~\mathrm{mg}$ . The water contact angle was measured using SL200B (Kino Company, USA) by the method of dynamic contact angle. The tensile test of the membrane was carried out with the TY8000A-500N electromechanical universal testing machine (Jiangsu Tianyuan Testing Equipment Co., Ltd) in accordance with the ASTMD638 standard. The sample was $4\mathrm{cm}\times0.5\mathrm{cm}$ . The speed was 10 $\mathrm{{N/min}}$ and each sample was tested for 5 times.
## 2.5. Silver ion leaching test
The composite membrane with a diameter of $5\mathrm{cm}$ was immersed in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water and placed in a constant temperature oscil­ lator to shake for 4, 8, 12, 24, and $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , respectively. An inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometer (ICP-MS) was used to test the ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ concentration in the leachate.
## 2.6. PM filtration measurement
The PM adsorption experiment was carried out in a self-assembled experimental device as shown in S1. The dust detector was the LD-5/J laser dust meter from Nanjing Trinyaer Environmental Protection Technology Co., Ltd. The PM particles were produced by burning mos­ quito repellent incense in the upper container. The prepared membrane was cut into $7\mathrm{cm}\times7$ cm square and sandwiched between upper and lower containers. The air in the upper container was drawn through the prepared membrane into the lower container using a vacuum pump at a flow rate of $2~\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{min}$ . The removal efficiency $R e$ was calculated via Eq. (1):
$$
R e=\frac{C_{a b o v e}-C_{b e l o w}}{C_{a b o v e}}\times100\%
$$
where $C_{a b o\nu e}$ was the PM concentration in the upper container $(|\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{m}^{3})$ and $C_{b e l o w}$ was the PM concentration in the lower container $(\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{m}^{3})$ .
## 2.7. Antibacterial test of $_{A g}$ -2MI and PLLA/Ag-2MI fiber membrane
E. coli and S. aureus were used to evaluate the antibacterial activity of
Ag-2MI and fiber membranes. The samples and glassware were sterilized by an ultraviolet lamp and a high-pressure steam sterilization pot before the antibacterial experiments, respectively. The target bacteria were cultivated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in Luria-Bertani broth until the bacteria concentra­ tion at approximately $10^{9}\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ . The concentration of bacterial sus­ pension was diluted with sterile water to $10^{5}\mathrm{{CFU/mL}}$ . Then, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of $10^{5}\mathrm{\CFU/mL}$ suspension was added into $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ water suspensions containing 0, 3, 6 and $9{\mathrm{~ppm~Ag{-}2M I}}$ , respectively. The bottles with the above mixtures were put on a constant temperature shaker at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and were shaken for different periods (10, 20, $30\mathrm{min}\mathrm{.}$ ). Subsequently, $1\mathrm{mL}$ of suspension after different shake period was pipetted and cultivated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in Luria-Bertani broth for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The antibacterial rate $(R)$ was calculated via Eq. (2) to assess the bactericidal effect:
$$
R\left(\%\right)=\left(N_{0}-N\right)/N_{0}\times100
$$
where $N_{0}$ and $N$ were the average number of viable bacteria on a reference sample without Ag-2MI (0 ppm) and the samples with 3, 6 and 9 ppm Ag-2MI after the antibacterial test, respectively.
$2~\mathrm{mL}$ of $10^{5}\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ suspension was put into $40~\mathrm{mL}$ water suspen­ sion containing the membrane discs of $5\mathrm{cm}$ diameter. The bottles with mixture were set on the shaker at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and shaken for different periods (4, 8, 12 h). Subsequently, $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ of suspension after different shake period was cultivated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The antibacterial rate (R) was obtained as the stated method above, where $N_{0}$ and $N$ are the average number of viable bacteria on a reference sample without discs and the sample containing discs after antibacterial tests.
