## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Morphology and structure of Ag-2MI
${\tt A g-}2{\tt M I}$ was synthesized through coprecipitation reaction of silver nitrate with 2-methylimidazole. Fig. 1a and b showed the SEM images of Ag-2MI and most of the synthesized Ag-2MI particles had an octahedral structure with a smooth face. As shown in Fig. 1c, there were sharp peaks at $2\Theta=16.89^{\circ}$ , $22.47^{\circ}$ , $27.98^{\circ}$ and $28.78^{\circ}$ , which corresponded to the reflection of (011), (200), (211) and (013) [26]. The FTIR of Ag-2MI was presented in Fig. 1d. The absorption peak observed at $3126~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was related to the asymmetric stretching vibration of unsaturated $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ , while the peak at $2904~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ reflected the asymmetric stretching vi­ bration of aliphatic C–H. The peak near $1613\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponded to the $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathrm{N}$ stretching vibration in the imidazole structure. The peaks near $1142\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $992\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $761\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1636\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were attributed to $\mathsf{C-N}$ stretching vibration, $\mathsf{C-N}$ bending vibration, $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ bending vibration and $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ stretching vibration, respectively. The stretching vibration absorption peak of ring skeleton of the imidazole appeared in the range of $1300{-}1465\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The peak at $427\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was related to the formation of the N-Ag-N coordination bond [27]. Fig. S2 in the supporting infor­ mation showed the TGA of Ag-2MI and three weight loss stages could be observed. The first stage with about $7.06~\mathrm{wt\%}$ weight loss started from $208^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and was mainly due to the release of residual solvent in the pores of Ag-2MI [28]. The second stage of weight loss began at $251^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{i}}$ which was caused by the collapse of the metal-organic framework. When the temperature exceeded $376^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the third stage of weight loss happened. The TGA results indicated that Ag-2MI could exist stably below $208^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ .
## 3.2. Antibacterial activities of Ag-2MI
In order to investigate the antibacterial activities of Ag-2MI, E. coli (Gram-negative bacteria) and S. aureus (Gram-positive bacteria) were used as the target bacteria and the minimum bactericidal concentration (MBC) of Ag-2MI was tested. The antibacterial results were shown in Fig. 2. As the concentration and contact time increased, the survival rate of $E$ . coli and S. aureus gradually decreased. When the contact time was $20\mathrm{min}$ , all the $E$ . coli in the 6 and 9 ppm experimental groups had been killed. The inhibition efficiency of Ag-2MI against S. aureus was slightly lower than that of $E$ . coli, but when the contact time was $30~\mathrm{min}$ , the antibacterial rate in the 9 ppm experiment group also reached $90.9\%$ . Karthik et al. [29] prepared Ag NPs and tested its antibacterial effect to E. coli. When the contact time was $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , the MBC was $40\mathrm{ppm}$ . Saied et al. [30] prepared a coordination compound $\mathsf{A g}(\mathsf{P T D M})\mathsf{N O}_{3}$ and studied its antibacterial properties. The MBC for E. coli and S. aureus were 18.2 $\upmu\mathrm{mol/L}$ and $36.5~\upmu\mathrm{mol/L}$ , respectively. In contrast, ${\tt A g-}2{\tt M I}$ had an excellent bactericidal ability, and a small amount of it could kill bacteria in a short period of time.
## 3.3. Surface morphology of the PLLA/Ag-2MI membranes
Fig. 3 showed the SEM and fiber diameters of PLLA, PAM1.0, PAM1.4 and PAM1.8. The fibers were crossed to form a threedimensional porous structure. Fig. 3a and b was the SEM and partial enlarged images of PLLA film. The surface of the film was smooth without bead string and the average diameter of fiber was about $910\mathrm{nm}$ . Fig. 3d–e, $g{\-}\mathrm{h}$ and $\mathbf{j-k}$ was images of fiber membranes with addition of Ag-2MI. Their average diameters in Fig. 3f, i and l were $600\mathrm{nm}$ , $530\mathrm{nm}$ and $520\mathrm{nm}$ , which were decreased in comparison with that of the PLLA membrane. The addition of Ag-2MI particles could reduce the surface energy and stabilize the electrospinning solution, which resulted in a reduction in the average diameters of the fibers [31–33]. It could be clearly seen that the Ag-2MI particles were attached to fibers and the fiber was well fused with Ag-2MI. Fig. 4 revealed the mapping images of PAM1.0, PAM1.4 and PAM1.8, respectively. The presence of the ele­ ments such as Ag and N further illustrated that Ag-2MI was incorporated in the fiber membranes. The Ag-2MI on the fibers provided abundant functional groups and open metal sites [32].
## 3.4. XRD, TGA and contact angle of the PLLA/Ag-2MI membranes
Fig. 5a showed the XRD pattern of Ag-2MI and the membranes. The PLLA membrane had a broad peak at $10{-}30^{\circ}$ due to its amorphous structure. The characteristic peaks of $\mathtt{A g-2M I}$ were located at $16.89^{\circ}$ , $22.47^{\circ}$ , $27.98^{\circ}$ and $28.78^{\circ}$ . It is worth noting that the XRD pattern of PAM1.0, PAM1.4 and PAM1.8 had a diffraction pattern similar to that of PLLA as well as the peaks of Ag-2MI. This indicated that $\mathsf{A g-}2\mathrm{MI}$ parti­ cles were successfully adhered to PLLA fibers without changing the structure of PLLA. Besides, the peak position of the (011) crystal plane for Ag-2MI in PAM1.8 was shifted from $16.89^{\circ}$ to $16.20^{\circ}$ , indicating the strong interaction between Ag-2MI and the PLLA fiber [33]. TGA was used to test the thermal stability of and the curves shown in Fig. 5b were obtained. The important TGA data of the fiber membranes were dis­ played in Table 2. It could be seen from Fig. 5b and Table 2 that both PLLA and PLLA/Ag-2MI membranes had one weight loss stage occurring between $278^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $390^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . When the mass fraction of Ag-2MI was 1 wt $\%$ , the initial decomposition temperature $(T_{i})$ was increased to $296^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ compared with $278^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ of pristine PLLA. The $T_{m a x}$ (temperature at maximum reaction rate) and $T_{f}$ (temperature of the mass loss end) of the membranes with ${\tt A g-}2{\tt M I}$ were also higher than that of pristine PLLA membranes. The improvement in the thermal stability was explained by the hindered mobility of the polymer chains and gas barrier effects preventing the degradation products from diffusing out of the compos­ ites by ${\tt A g-}2{\tt M I}$ crystals [34,35]. Fig. 5c displayed the water contact angles of the membranes. It could be seen from Fig. 5c that the addition of $\mathsf{A g-}2\mathrm{MI}$ enhanced the contact angle of the membranes. The contact angle of PAM1.8 was $109.62^{\circ}$ while the value of pristine PLLA was $88.69^{\circ}$ . The presence of porous Ag-2MI particles on the membranes and the decrease in the diameter of fiber contributed to the formation of hydrophobic surfaces [36], which meant that air was more likely to stay in the gap between fiber, and the dripping water would stay on the airfilled microstructure space instead of directly contacting the fiber interface and could not penetrate the fiber [37,38].
## 3.5. Mechanical properties of the PLLA/Ag-2MI membranes
The electrospinning fiber membrane was a kind of nonwoven fabric and its mechanical properties were affected by the composition, struc­ ture and inter-fiber interaction of a single fiber [39]. Fig. 6 exhibited the stress-strain curves of the membranes. It could be seen that the tensile stress of the fiber membrane increased sharply under a small strain at first, and then increased slowly with the strain. At the beginning of the
Ag La1
stretching process, most of the fibers in the film hardly moved due to the binding force between them [40,41]. When most of the fibers reached a taut state, the interaction points between the fibers were destroyed and the fibers would be oriented along the stretching direction if they were stretched continuously. As the stretching process continued, most of the fibers were pulled off and the fiber membrane was broken macroscop­ ically. Fig. 6b summarized the changes in the tensile strength and elongation at break of the membrane with Ag-2MI. The addition of Ag
2MI improved the tensile strength and elongation at break of the com­ posite fiber membranes. The tensile strength and the elongation at break of PAM1.8 were increased by nearly $64\%$ and $68\%$ compared with that of pure PLLA membrane. The reason for the improvement was owing to reinforcing effects of Ag-2MI crystals [42].
## 3.6. The PM filtration performance of PLLA/Ag-2MI fiber membranes
The filtration effects of the prepared membrane on PM could be directly observed in Fig. 7a. When the PM concentration was as high as $5000~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}/\mathrm{m}^{3}$ , the PM concentration in the air after filtration through the membrane was only $106\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{m}^{3}$ . As the concentration reached $2820\upmu\mathrm{g}/$ ${\mathfrak{m}}^{3}$ , the PM concentration in the air passing through the membrane was only $3\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{m}^{3}$ . It was noted that when the concentration reached $275\upmu\mathrm{g}/$ $\mathbf{m}^{3}$ , no PM was detected. This proved that the prepared membrane had a good filtration effect on PM. In order to evaluate the filtration performance of the prepared membrane further, we compared it with the melt-blown PP non-woven fabrics. The thickness of the prepared film was controlled at $0.036\ \mathrm{mm}$ , while the thickness of the commercially available melt blown cloth was about $0.10\mathrm{mm}$ as shown in Fig. 7b. The removal efficiency of the films on PM2.5 and PM10 at different moments was shown in Fig. 7c and d, respectively. It could be seen that although the prepared film was much thinner than the melt blown cloth, it had a better filtration effect. The removal efficiency of PM2.5 and PM10 within $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ was $99.61\%-100\%$ and $99.84\%-100\%$ for PAM1.8. The effect of adding Ag-2MI on filtration could also be seen in Fig. 7c and d. After addition of Ag-2MI, the PM removal efficiency of the composite membrane was better than that of the pure PLLA membrane. The high specific surface area and porous characteristics of $\mathsf{A g-}2\mathrm{MI}$ made the membrane have a higher specific surface and provide more adsorption sites for PM [31,43]. It was reported that the dipole moment of PM particles was about 3.5D. When Ag-2MI with high dipole moment (3.61D) was added, strong electrostatic interaction between Ag-2MI and PM would be stimulated, thereby improving the filtration efficiency [44,45].
Fig. 8 showed the photos of melt blown cloth as well as the prepared membranes before and after adsorption. It could be seen that all the membranes had changed from white before adsorption to brownish yellow after adsorption, indicating that the fiber membrane had indeed captured the PM particles. The SEM images of PLLA and the prepared membranes after PM capture were manifested in Fig. 8b–e and the PM particles were clearly on the composite fiber membranes.
## 3.7. Antibacterial properties of PLLA/Ag-2MI fiber membranes
Fig. 9a showed the number of colonies in the petri dish after the fiber membranes were shaken in E. coli and S. aureus suspensions for 4, 8, and $12\mathrm{h}$ , respectively. The number of colonies with pristine PLLA membrane as a control did not change significantly, while the number of colonies on PAM1.0, PAM1.4 and PAM1.8 decreased evidently. The relationship of inhibition rate with contact time was displayed in Fig. 9b and c. For E. coli, when the contact time was $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , the antibacterial rate of PAM1.8 reached $99.85\%$ and the antibacterial rate exceeded $99.99\%$ after $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ contact. For S. aureus, when the contact time was $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , the antibacterial rate of PAM1.8 was $87.50\%$ and the antibacterial rate reached $99.99\%$ after $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Fig. 9c–f showed the SEM images of PLLA and PAM1.8 after shaking in E. coli and S. aureus suspensions for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . It could be seen clearly that the bacteria adhered to the fiber membranes. Most of E. coli and S. aureus on PAM1.8 were ruptured and the surface of the bacteria shrank, while the bacteria on the PLLA fiber membrane were intact. The antibacterial effect of the membranes in this work compared with others was shown in Table 3 and the results demonstrated that our membranes with low loading of Ag-2MI had a good bactericidal effect.
The antibacterial properties of the PLLA/Ag-2MI fiber membranes were mainly achieved through two ways, namely the adsorption of the membranes to bacteria and the release of antibacterial ion in ${\tt A g-}2{\tt M I}$ [50]. On the one hand, the three-dimensional network structure of the fiber membranes facilitated the adsorption of bacteria, and on the other hand, when Ag-2MI touched the surface of the bacteria [51], Ag-2MI collapsed and released ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ quickly. ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ penetrated into the cell mem­ brane to destroy the integrity of the cell membrane and combined with the -OH, -COOH, -CO-, $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\ell}}_{-\mathrm{NH_{2}}}$ , and -SH functional groups in the cell or DNA, resulting in death of cell [52,53]. The antibacterial performance at this time was mainly proportional to the release of ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ . As indicated in Fig. S3, the ${\mathbf{A}}{\mathbf{g}}^{+}$ leaching concentration from membranes increased with time. In addition to the role of metal ions, the imidazole-based organic ligands also had certain antibacterial ability [54].
## 4. Conclusions
The ${\tt A g-}2{\tt M I}$ particles were prepared and incorporated in the PLLA electrospinning fiber membranes. When the Ag-2MI content was 1.8 wt $\%$ , the tensile strength of the PLLA/Ag-2MI composite electrospun membrane was increased by nearly $64\%$ compared with pristine PLLA film, and the elongation at break increased from $28\%$ of the PLLA membrane to $68\%$ . The filtration performance of the composite membrane with Ag-2MI was better than pristine PLLA film and melt blown cloth. When $1.8\mathrm{wt\%}$ of Ag-2MI was added, the removal efficiency is $99.61\%-100\%$ and $99.84\%-100\%$ for $\mathrm{PM}2.5$ and PM10 within 20 min, respectively. The PAM1.8 had an antibacterial rate of $99.99\%$ for E. coli and S. aureus after $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Therefore, the prepared film with good antibacterial and filtration performances could be potentially used as filtration materials in the field of masks and air conditioner instead of melt blown cloth, and solved the problem of non-degradability and nonfunctionalization of melt blown cloth.
